#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213833} :activity/id #uuid "251bed00-5af3-5da2-8724-ccbb6ef78316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134 :topic/id #uuid "8238e088-d8cf-5b16-b7de-d1a376704040" :topic/math-academy-id 6092} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214213 :tutorial/content "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions. \n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2,$ and\n\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5.$\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x.$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d.\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x.$\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials." :tutorial/id #uuid "93d3fd0c-7714-59b4-970d-29dc3c5d7545" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13405 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d954cce-ceac-584d-81b8-41621685f324" :step/math-academy-id 476435 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213834}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214223 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f.\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x,$ we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\na = 1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb = 5.\n$$\n\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nc = 4.\n$$\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients. **  It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "ce7ce256-8fad-563a-81d9-48084c6f798e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13433 :tutorial/title "Equating Coefficients of Quadratic Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "69793601-905b-53e3-a371-2d7a2880b2ec" :step/math-academy-id 476436 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213003 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212754 :question/id #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" :question/math-academy-id "e-18859"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297187}] :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "738997c9-869e-5314-a969-85caff7c6928" :step/math-academy-id 476437 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213004 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212755 :question/id #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-18860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518105}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "11885930-6dfe-5dec-bf2e-868ee09bcb73" :step/math-academy-id 476440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212756 :question/id #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" :question/math-academy-id "e-18861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512877}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"} :step/id #uuid "5555353e-7091-5f3a-a5a5-2e6f6a73e504" :step/math-academy-id 476443}] :activity/title "Equating Polynomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186213259}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518253} :activity/id #uuid "013fe3a7-9b4e-5fe1-9bf4-78ad7af81a4d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134 :topic/id #uuid "8238e088-d8cf-5b16-b7de-d1a376704040" :topic/math-academy-id 6092} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9100f91-fc1e-5c98-a41b-4ed3a7acfa72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210532} {:answer/id #uuid "ed823b62-086b-52e2-9169-be2b4891c218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210533} {:answer/id #uuid "f302cf8a-2800-5e54-a999-ea8d52aa8520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210534} {:answer/id #uuid "2d33a223-1b57-5f84-a71f-c47693561011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210535} {:answer/id #uuid "6a23987b-c061-59bf-85c0-0f6fd3a3635e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186210536} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fd7528a-eee1-5d01-b7fb-cfa05b48f0f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186210531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b5a718a-0e39-5107-8c8d-d7ed4a7ca3e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288053" :question/problem "The equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+ax+b={x}^{2}+10x+24\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $a,$ and on the right-hand side it is $10.$ So, we have \n\n$$\na=10.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $b,$ and on the right-hand side it is $24.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nb=24.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =10+24 \\\\  & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4e57b915-7c23-5742-9100-719d0b89ea07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190cbee0-7af2-576e-98ad-a582cb732f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517534} {:answer/id #uuid "1bdf64b3-5ded-5be3-9039-ad4566118032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517539} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1157a8-572c-5cf4-86f8-7272bd09d639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517719} {:answer/id #uuid "924ee794-d6a4-593d-a93d-b2c314a2846d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517764} {:answer/id #uuid "a181b8a1-35c7-56bc-a5a7-f50443574e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517764} :answer-field/id #uuid "825ec111-8fef-5db1-a9c2-27ab6f1d4b42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "090c9908-d9d1-5e59-8b6d-0af06c1b251a" :question/math-academy-id "q-288455" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+ux+v={x}^{2}-x+1\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $u$ and $v$ are constants. What is the value of $u+v?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $u,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nu=-1.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $v,$ and on the right-hand side it is $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb0762a1-82e2-5ea9-ba93-22a0ea8c9976"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a0ec0e-0617-53dc-ab33-c0b23c91fb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295559} {:answer/id #uuid "78d3896b-7cc7-55d9-93d6-eb6dd98142c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295927} {:answer/id #uuid "b29faf52-a539-5828-8e4c-ae2fdf02df18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296209} {:answer/id #uuid "b806ba99-6dbd-5acb-9131-b1acf13afbcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296231} {:answer/id #uuid "ea97feea-c170-5169-adc6-b127b06a5058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296231} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd28e05e-209e-5653-9366-8140063b7d1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ffcc7b45-1246-5e48-a7e0-93379ccd5076" :question/math-academy-id "q-288451" :question/problem "The equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+mx+c={x}^{2}+7x-5\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $m$ and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $m+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $m,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nm=7.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $c,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-5.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nc=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+c & =7+(-5) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f3b2b462-1f6b-56b5-930b-3e10e7711b0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518312}}] :activity/title "Equating Polynomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517475}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199405} :activity/id #uuid "5a962fb0-35f3-53a6-b932-b8fc6eb20898" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048355 :topic/id #uuid "97fcc1c4-2745-5d7e-9f8b-24c174883d6f" :topic/math-academy-id 4361} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199546 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how Venn diagrams can be used to calculate probabilities such as $P(A\\cup B)$ (the union of two events) and $P(A\\cap B)$ (the intersection of two events) for events $A$ and $B$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include event complements.\n\nFor example, consider the event $A \\cap B'$. How might we depict this event on a Venn diagram?\n\nThe idea is to sketch $A$ and $B'$ separately and then find their intersection, as follows:\n\n- The event $A$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/ab/abf925fb7b1e245a27f54851d9065ef1f25281d48a094c5152c586229df0efd4.png)\n\n- The event $B'$ is represented by the shaded region below. It encompasses everything in the sample space $\\mathcal S$ that is **not** in $B$.\n\n![](images/c7/c7b8767c53739f3f44cc99eee7d1cc24621ab346247c083e9624f8a10fc512e2.png)\n\n- Finally, to find $A \\cap B'$, we take the intersection (or common overlap) of the two shaded regions shown above. This gives the following:\n\n![](images/74/74d29171067d70aeadf6eeb520397b0f82b9279ea4981b0f670b0f9a83213a6a.png)\n\nLet's follow a similar process to depict a union of events on a Venn diagram." :tutorial/id #uuid "4bd15252-5112-5483-933b-d799f106d08a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8172 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9fd6356f-9755-518f-977e-b262e57a2132" :step/math-academy-id 55265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199406}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198144 :question/id #uuid "8df05d2a-b78f-5515-90e6-27c0264042db" :question/math-academy-id "e-13076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace27779-e2c4-5bbf-87bc-7e5227a6e5f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050587}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198767}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions on Venn Diagrams Corresponding to Complements"} :step/id #uuid "7ef4e24f-93e5-5026-ad4b-dc74096d8a27" :step/math-academy-id 55266 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198141 :question/id #uuid "225ed6ce-5b8f-5570-85fe-f093c8c7632c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874dfb35-398e-5ed7-8a76-f0011569e6a1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233592}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Complement Using a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "a55c8c4e-50f2-5bfb-8cf8-f98b2797612b" :step/math-academy-id 55269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198452 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198142 :question/id #uuid "c613ab13-9671-528c-9e10-1f05ea0b0878" :question/math-academy-id "e-13073"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cd170a0-47b2-5747-b7a1-13521b841e37" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199009}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space"} :step/id #uuid "1033361e-36a7-546a-9e14-b22db6eac276" :step/math-academy-id 55272}] :activity/title "Computing Probabilities of Events Containing Complements Using Venn Diagrams" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198529}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213857} :activity/id #uuid "b8af5a55-31af-5f64-8ca5-6aa369cb9ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048758 :topic/id #uuid "bc0baf51-6352-5e73-9783-27ae4241c004" :topic/math-academy-id 6185} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214235 :tutorial/content "Rationalizing the denominator works similarly for algebraic expressions as it does for numerical ones. When the denominator contains two terms and includes radicals, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the *conjugate* of the denominator to eliminate the radical.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{x}$, all we have to do is flip the sign between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1-\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/((1 + \\sqrt{x})(1 - \\sqrt{x})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{x})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8dde46d5-669e-5c3a-b471-7541b1824862" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13464 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c88995e-61d0-5dad-a452-dff7902b363d" :step/math-academy-id 478497 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212794 :question/id #uuid "659e7fd3-3502-5a47-a54c-3d0c33150d73" :question/math-academy-id "e-18928"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9139c75-1189-5b78-9d82-023f5208a490" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049235}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213827}] :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"} :step/id #uuid "501a0f87-638b-5be9-988b-a3d9207c5314" :step/math-academy-id 478498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212795 :question/id #uuid "dfb7701e-d11b-52a0-97fb-8363b1ff0901" :question/math-academy-id "e-18929"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa5d982f-ff13-5a31-9cd3-d5847ea2790c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497392}] :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing a Binomial Within the Radical"} :step/id #uuid "494c1398-1801-5014-8bc0-39d88af8010b" :step/math-academy-id 478501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213019 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212796 :question/id #uuid "4bcf9ec5-b5a1-5ab2-bb9f-f22280e072ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-18930"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43395cdf-3e16-5147-87fb-371193078b4a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497414}] :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing a Denominator With Two Radicals"} :step/id #uuid "bb9fd774-ee90-5072-becc-85b80612fb62" :step/math-academy-id 478504}] :activity/title "Further Rationalizing Denominators of Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186213279}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497422} :activity/id #uuid "3a3a3cd6-f7f4-55d3-b9f0-51b6efa29ace" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048758 :topic/id #uuid "bc0baf51-6352-5e73-9783-27ae4241c004" :topic/math-academy-id 6185} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed6f177-f0a7-51b5-91f1-45cc3525ec42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{w-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496724} {:answer/id #uuid "297af7c5-9d20-5ec8-b43e-b61d43129d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6})}{w-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496749} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7e0d94-a5f9-5adf-866f-e3b8e6f6df78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}}{w-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496762} {:answer/id #uuid "89c3d947-dd0c-55f3-b1d7-b34cdac78a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{w-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496946} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e1d989-f66b-52e3-97f0-bbb5658af8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{w-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496724} :answer-field/id #uuid "9306fa45-f979-577d-88b6-95185ddf9d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7189aac-304f-5339-9ec2-dd1037933cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85407" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{2w}-\\sqrt{6})(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{2w})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{2w-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6})}{2(w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2w}+\\sqrt{6}}{w-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "09ba30a2-1f6a-5308-b39e-d7963fd4bd09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59c0abeb-efea-5346-a12d-6aceb41e5d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2c+5)(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496852} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6d0a3a-7076-5fdb-9621-e69c7e602ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2c+5)(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{c-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496858} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4043b8-d542-56a8-94c3-4a171b378921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496889} {:answer/id #uuid "d09d9917-b956-5f74-8457-7b67b79b18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2c+5)(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{c+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497090} {:answer/id #uuid "e3220828-f713-5b43-83e7-461b5c071967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2c+5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497145} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b21d62e-53c1-50ce-9ab2-68ee95cacfa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b52a407e-c533-5755-83ae-d5bfaf9c5559" :question/math-academy-id "q-111090" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4{c}^{2}-25}{\\sqrt{2c-1}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2c-1}-2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2c-1}-2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{c}^{2}-25}{\\sqrt{2c-1}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4{c}^{2}-25}{\\sqrt{2c-1}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2c-1}-2}{\\sqrt{2c-1}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(4{c}^{2}-25)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{(\\sqrt{2c-1}+2)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(4{c}^{2}-25)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{(\\sqrt{2c-1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2c+5)(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{2c-1-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2c+5)(2c-5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{2c-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2c+5)\\cancel{(2c-5)}(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)}{\\cancel{2c-5}} & = \\\\ (2c+5)(\\sqrt{2c-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5f2f1ea6-264c-5162-b466-fa4e13fff556" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b65670a-a896-5610-82ac-5751ceff85c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496715} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef52864-87e6-5b65-8775-27fca6c3a068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496832} {:answer/id #uuid "82b190a0-3b5e-5790-9137-dada26dc5d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496925} {:answer/id #uuid "cff26320-aaef-57fe-a31f-7c3b21af878a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497087} {:answer/id #uuid "d2128cd1-c593-59ee-8c09-21ce7bd84ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497096} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48e28cb-69c5-5ec3-b0b0-45cd22625ed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "48232a69-9972-5f32-be1d-adbffa500e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-296049" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{x+3}-\\sqrt{x+5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}}{\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(\\sqrt{x+3})^{2}-(\\sqrt{x+5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{(x+3)-(x+5)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})}{-2} & = \\\\ -6(\\sqrt{x+3}+\\sqrt{x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ec5e5a5-7a2a-583a-957a-7ba050ce0b2e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24902765-f87a-52d2-8aed-b72916532c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496737} {:answer/id #uuid "59034b89-95f0-5bee-a19c-3cc11e74e14e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496848} {:answer/id #uuid "7afbc696-174b-5aa6-a361-d4fb1513898f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496916} {:answer/id #uuid "beea4dc9-8883-552c-ac78-4a1888f63aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497047} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c5c02f-1750-5fe2-84d3-9f069116c389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497063} :answer-field/id #uuid "352e22d3-3322-5945-aacd-bbb5be700ed3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c076968-69eb-5008-a15f-3101901a7753" :question/math-academy-id "q-111085" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{q+1}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{q+1}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{q+1}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{q+1}+2}{\\sqrt{q+1}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{q+1}-2)(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{q+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q+1-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{q+1}+2)}{q-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aa626a1f-98b3-5cf9-9aea-f591b59129ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb361c6-6dd5-552c-a427-a36cf070b655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{w(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{w+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496679} {:answer/id #uuid "4881d5c1-1bbc-586e-9272-b79e718a7ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{w(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496822} {:answer/id #uuid "58b04e7f-48fb-5d60-9894-e699ad85fce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496845} {:answer/id #uuid "59ba9695-6e1c-5180-8ee9-758ebad42ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496851} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc00c71-a5e7-57f3-b1b5-fd9dc5eab57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496991} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc4d4eb-b093-5f42-bf15-0372a2d6d50b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58d0d2af-1f8e-5b5b-ada1-d6636471e4c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-111092" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2w+3}+1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2w+3}+1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{w+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}-1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1}{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{(\\sqrt{2w+3}-1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{(\\sqrt{2w+3})^{2}-{1}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2w+3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(w+1)(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2w+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(w+1)}(\\sqrt{2w+3}+1)}{2\\cancel{(w+1)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2w+3}+1}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "af17654d-170c-57ac-970d-2a1adbb811ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497446}}] :activity/title "Further Rationalizing Denominators of Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496646}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213927} :activity/id #uuid "6b2744f1-6df8-58f8-95c6-02c72762cf74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050530 :topic/id #uuid "508f9d54-06c2-56d9-a727-550fad84366a" :topic/math-academy-id 6219} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214233 :tutorial/content "We often use linear functions in real-world situations to describe relationships between quantities.\n\nFor example, suppose a writer is tracking their progress while typing a draft. They observe that\n\n- after $2$ minutes, they have typed $300$ words, and\n- after $5$ minutes, they have typed $750$ words.\n\nWe can build a linear function to model this situation.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of minutes, and $g(x)$ represent the number of words typed after $x$ minutes.\nThen, the linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nfor some slope $m$ and value $b$, which we need to find.\n\nFrom the two observations, we know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 300) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 750)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m$ using the slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{750 - 300}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{450}{3} \\\\\n&= 150\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the writer is typing at a rate of $150$ words per minute.\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 150x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 300)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n300 &= 150 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n300 &= 300 + b \\\\\nb &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means the starting number of words is $0$ - the writer began typing from scratch.\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = 150x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75f58e28-fe24-58b2-857a-9c0b7070ccd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13461 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3cac7a4f-be5f-5268-8b3d-21f275556a87" :step/math-academy-id 485545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212788 :question/id #uuid "aaf7aedd-ad76-5b8d-bb6a-3925f8d41006" :question/math-academy-id "e-18920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "098734a8-c226-5bc7-b174-7c0d794e986c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050216}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213557}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Linear Functions Using Slope-Intercept Form"} :step/id #uuid "2b04c002-e8c7-54a8-8375-43fcf5243adc" :step/math-academy-id 485546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212793 :question/id #uuid "604f7244-01c2-597e-9590-b7ab3965d816" :question/math-academy-id "e-18926"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3da0835-3969-564f-b987-a019aca8fb55" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213571}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Functions From Tabular Data"} :step/id #uuid "54f29bf8-3b84-513a-a72e-97f67a67acbc" :step/math-academy-id 485549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214234 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, when modeling real-life situations with linear functions, the independent variable does not start at zero.\n\nFor example, suppose a puzzle enthusiast is working on a large jigsaw puzzle. Their progress follows this pattern:\n\n- On the first day, they place $50$ pieces.\n- They add $30$ more pieces on each of the following days.\n\nWith this, we can construct a linear function $P$ for the total number of puzzle pieces placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's break down the pattern to understand it:\n\n- On day $d=1$, they place $P=50$ pieces.\n- On day $d=2$, they place $P=50+30=80$ pieces in total.\n- On day $d=3$, they place $P=50+30+30=110$ pieces in total.\n- $\\cdots$\n\nAnd so on.\n\nNotice that the amount added each day after the first is always $30$. So the total number of pieces placed includes\n\n- the initial $50$ pieces placed on the first day, and\n- $30$ pieces for each additional day *beyond the first*. Since the first $50$ pieces are placed on day $1$, the number of *additional* pieces placed by day $d$ equals\n$30(d - 1)$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of pieces $P$ placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(d) &= 50 + 30(d - 1) \\\\\n&= 50 + 30d - 30 \\\\\n&= 30d + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f6ca0c8-4565-5090-8352-bc4db72075a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13462 :tutorial/title "Linear Functions With Alternative Domains"} :step/id #uuid "08e9ff75-663e-532d-b5f9-2d4420b6160f" :step/math-academy-id 485553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212789 :question/id #uuid "4fb71375-cea2-5364-b809-c844c462a1a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-18921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "410a9971-d4c0-57ff-b936-58621a2313b5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050908}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512876}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Functions With Alternative Domains"} :step/id #uuid "656e8d0e-9a81-5393-bc19-285f65f71c5c" :step/math-academy-id 485554}] :activity/title "Constructing Linear Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186213298}]}
